{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S211-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c9e5cbd677f581aaaed288aecdff11cdf0dcea909ca130848a94ccf11e7b969d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 delays taking the won seat for an announcement; A3 requires public naming.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 corrects B2's anonymous recognition; A5 scales the ceremony to B4's train-seat objection.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants A seated; B4 challenges the ceremony's proportionality.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 absorbs B4's objection into a smaller but still compulsory announcement.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a coin toss and window seat; A3 supplies the passenger audience.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'suit the occasion perfectly' earns its formality from B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I won our coin toss. Please announce who sits by the window.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You do. Sit down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"With my name. The other passengers weren't watching.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's a train seat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then a brief announcement will suit the occasion perfectly.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S211 makes an awarded result incomplete until recognition and ritual authorize its practical consequence.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple vanity would seek admiration without making ceremony a condition of proceeding.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 requests an announcement before sitting, and A5 preserves that requirement despite B4's objection.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative recruits a public audience; single-b negotiates a private ritual with an exhausted fellow player.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: both partner replies reshape the required recognition. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings and spoken ABABA gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The delay before sitting is implied rather than explicitly stated, and A5 heightens persistence more than consequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S211-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"cb3a43d8fd9c95bac599cc0b622fd395d40fd739e3aeafa473420b90908deff2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 blocks the next hand until a handshake; A3 treats missing recognition as an incomplete record.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's six wins to count five recognitions; A5 substitutes a nod for B4's tired hand.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 establish the opponent's accumulated fatigue, which A5 accommodates.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 extends ceremonial requirements to a bodily concession prompted by B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes repeated card hands; A3 supplies a precise recognition deficit.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's unreliable records and A5's ceremonial nod follow the same sincere logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 93\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before the next hand of cards, we need the winner's handshake.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You've won six hands.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And we've recognised only five. This is how records become unreliable.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My hand is tired.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"A nod will do if you make it ceremonial.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S211 requires a completed recognition ritual before the next competitive activity can begin.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Gloating repeats superiority; this behavior instead insists that each result receive its missing formal acknowledgment.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 counts unrecognized wins, and A5 accepts reduced physical effort while retaining ceremony.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike single-a's public announcement, this scene bargains over the minimum private ritual a tired opponent can supply.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged because B4's practical difficulty directly generates A5's accommodation. Internal rereading leaves the original ratings unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 reduces physical scale, so its development depends on playing the ceremonial condition with conviction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S212-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6d8b9ae3c01e32776155495ecf50114d1e2eb60e7b10ea966f759fd1edf39649\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A3 worries about commitment and A5 about historical recognition, rather than elimination of the future.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's sharing proposal, but A5's plaque adds a new prestige concern after B4.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a workable lunch arrangement; B4 merely restates the ordinary situation.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 introduces historical importance without increasing A1's one-lunch stakes.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes picnic sandwiches and B2 offers an immediately playable solution.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 is concise, but its plaque joke is detached from the elimination premise.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 53\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Choose our picnic sandwich carefully. Pick badly and this is our last picnic.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can share both.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then both sandwiches get a chance to eliminate us.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll taste them first. You can trust my choice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Now one bite decides whether I can ever trust you with lunch again.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S212 turns a minor selection into an irreversible elimination of future possibilities.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Grandiosity makes lunch historically important; sudden-death thinking makes a mistaken lunch choice terminate future participation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 seeks a plaque; revised A1 ends future picnics and A5 puts all future lunch trust on one bite.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This revision threatens a shared routine and absorbs B's helpful tasting offer; single-b threatens membership in a friendship.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the picnic setting. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's trust-based solution makes A5 stake future trust on the taste test; all dialogue gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The elimination language is deliberately conspicuous; actors must keep A's concern sincere rather than announce a metaphor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S212-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"31cd33f9a10bfd9beddffb68ce7a22e19e2ece8807ab25864c50a37129ef22da\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes an emoji end the friendship, but A5 narrows into ordinary anxiety about offending someone.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 turns B2's correction into evidence against affection; A5 uses B4's cake.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers reassurance, but B4 supplies little personal stake beyond identifying the emoji.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 specifies a possible insult without developing A1's terminal friendship consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes composing a birthday text and A5 identifies candle count.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'first affection was defective' follows directly from the proposed correction.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 78\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Help me choose an emoji for Nina's birthday. One wrong face and I'm out of her life.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Send a cake. That's what I sent.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then she has your cake to compare mine against. Only one of us survives.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She replied with a heart. Copy mine.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The winning cake is taken. Now I need one that beats a heart.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S212 treats each tiny messaging choice as a round that can eliminate A from the friendship's future.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Social perfectionism seeks the ideal message; this stance assumes a single losing choice permanently removes someone.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 converts B2's successful suggestion into a one-survivor contest, and A5 makes B4's heart a new threshold.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a escalates a shared practical solution into lost trust; this alternative turns a helpful friend's success into a competitive cutoff.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the existing birthday-text setting, naming the recipient. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's demonstrated success creates A5's higher survival threshold; all dialogue gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's terminal stakes rely on A1 and A3; isolated from the exchange, it could sound merely competitive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S213-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a4c48c71bd84a6990e61580df89fe2d86370d7cfe70b0d46b006eb2c1d81e990\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 welcomes losing and A3 invents a comeback story, but removing a team name barely raises the challenge.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains B2's queried tactic; A5 rejects B4's studying to preserve dramatic pressure.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions effectiveness and B4 seeks an actual improvement in quiz performance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 protects existing ignorance instead of adding a clear new challenge.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 locates the loss at a village quiz and proposes returning next week.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's achievement description is wordy relative to the slight nameless-team premise.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 69\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Last in the village quiz! Next week let's enter as a team of two.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We had six people and still lost.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. Imagine the comeback with four fewer brains.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll come if we study first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes! We'll study, then enter the county final. This defeat deserves a bigger audience.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S213 welcomes a setback as the reason to attempt a harder comeback with a more exciting story.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Saving face excuses a loss; this stance actively uses the loss to justify greater difficulty and another attempt.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 reduces the team after losing, and A5 uses B4's preparation offer to attempt a larger competition.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative recruits a teammate and accepts preparation; single-b independently turns cake failures into taller redemption attempts.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised in the existing quiz setting, adding a proposed county final. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's study condition enables A5's larger challenge; all dialogue gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The county final is a comic aspiration; the dialogue does not establish whether entry requires qualification.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S213-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"92a6446d0fbeb324836116b4ba8ae61854a03ff62ae78d5b7eb3c02538fc5c49\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 responds to collapse with three tiers; A3 makes failure an origin story; A5 proposes four.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 transforms B2's one-tier failure into narrative value; A5 answers B4's three-tier collapse.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 credibly question competence, though B's own stake in the cake is unspecified.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B4's hypothetical larger failure makes A5 demand an even taller redemption.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a collapsed cake, next year, and three tiers.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's origin story and A5's burden of redemption convert structural failure into narrative ambition.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"My cake collapsed. Next year I'll make one with three tiers.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You couldn't manage one.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. One tier is now my origin story.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"What if three collapse?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then four will have an extraordinary burden of redemption.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S213 converts each failure into enthusiasm for a harder retry and a more consequential comeback story.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Blind optimism predicts success; this stance also finds a larger failure exciting because it improves the next attempt's story.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A5 accepts B4's possible collapse and uses it to justify four tiers rather than denying the risk.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This scene escalates a maker's private ambition through hypothetical failure; single-a negotiates a teammate's participation and preparation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged because each new scale of failure generates the next scale of challenge. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings and dialogue gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B's connection to the cake remains open, and the final phrasing needs conversational delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S214-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"25ace5a52d55a3978d67bd6d1407f68c3661b0e806533e5942f938ee29a29eac\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 accepts the trophy while asking insurance costs; A3 tests the financial content of B2's honour.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2 precisely, but A5's custody question is muddled after B4 offers returning the trophy.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends symbolic value and B4 offers an escape from the burden.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 questions the prize again without clarifying the new consequence of donation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes trophy insurance at home and A3 supplies a collapsing shelf.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's repeated honour is earned, but A5's 'won custody of' obscures the practical question.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 76\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lovely trophy. Who pays to insure it while it's in my house?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The winner. We can display it here if you'd rather.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Does that transfer the insurance bill to the club?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yes. We'd ask you to polish it before open days.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"How many open days? I'd like to compare the insurance with the unpaid shifts.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S214 accepts the win while tracing who receives its benefits and bears its continuing costs.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Refusing an honour rejects its value outright; this stance audits its practical terms before deciding what the win delivers.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 checks the bill transfer offered by B2, and A5 prices the labor newly disclosed by B4.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This version compares two custody arrangements with a helpful club representative; single-b exposes the labor hidden inside public recognition.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised in the trophy-and-club setting. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 5, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 93. B4 replaces insurance with polishing, causing A5's cost comparison; all dialogue gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final line is an accounting question more than a sharp punchline, which suits the practical stance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S214-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"72b53012716986ad35ba66840a23106ec01b5ef4d7b66c9bae2d85db43889675\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 accepts the garden prize's visitor consequence; A3 asks who performs the work.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 probes B2's recognition claim; A5 infers the value of losing from B4's assignment of labor.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 promotes the honor while B4 reveals that the winner supplies the work.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's duty into a comparison of first and second prizes.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 fixes Sunday public visits; A3 names gate opening and cleanup.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'second prize is a day off' compresses the revealed cost into a playable question.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So winning the best-garden prize means the public visits on Sunday?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's wonderful recognition.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Who opens the gate and cleans up afterwards?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The winner.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I see. The second prize is a day off?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S214 examines a conceded victory by following its advertised benefit through to the person paying in labor.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Sour grapes disputes the prize's worth after losing; A has won and asks what its obligations actually are.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 accepts the prize, A3 asks about concrete duties, and A5 compares outcomes only after B4 assigns those duties.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a negotiates alternative costs with a helpful custodian; this scene questions a promoter's equation of recognition with benefit.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: the final question depends on B4's disclosure and develops the cost inquiry. Internal rereading confirms the original ratings.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B2 is a familiar promotional dodge, and the final question could become sarcasm if A loses sincere curiosity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S215-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5d7f63848fb572d0a6c600f05801578c2fc16716f974442309323c6d5a619e3d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes the half-point govern leaving, but A5 explicitly settles for accurate defeat rather than decisive advantage.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's large loss to remove resistance; A5 directly answers B4's unchanged winner.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 try to end a futile dispute while A1 prevents departure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates a precision principle without increasing the half-point's control over choices.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies a scorer and missing half-point; B2 specifies a thirty-point loss.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'lose accurately' is compact and logically earned, despite its adjacent-card emphasis.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"We're not leaving the quiz until my missing half-point is restored.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We lost by thirty. I've booked dinner.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Move the booking. That half-point decides whether this evening was worth coming out for.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The scorer can email a correction tomorrow.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then book dinner tomorrow. I won't choose our celebration restaurant without the final half-point.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S215 selects one tiny scoring detail and makes it govern decisions far beyond its numerical significance.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Accuracy for its own sake seeks a correct record; this stance makes the tiny correction decisive for unrelated practical choices.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 values accurate defeat; revised A3 moves dinner and A5 postpones choosing a restaurant over the same half-point.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This scene lets a tiny score dictate a companion's evening; single-b argues that one cherry should decide a judging category.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised with an explicit quiz and dinner booking. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's delayed correction causes A5 to delay dinner too; all dialogue gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Calling dinner a celebration after a large loss requires A to believe the half-point changes the evening's meaning.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S215-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6e050b405a989ba6a8e51714623fe675381c9de283bf753552e70a6820413a46\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 declares one extra cherry decisive; A3 and A5 preserve its primacy against competing criteria.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's flavour criterion; A5 uses B4's burnt cake to increase the cherry's weight.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends the category and B4 supplies a substantive reason A should lose.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes worse overall quality increase the chosen detail's importance.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies an extra cherry in a cake competition; B4 adds the burnt result.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's three-word rebuttal sets up A5's inflated statistical justification.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"My cake had one more cherry. That should settle the category.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The category was flavour.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Cherries have flavour.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yours was burnt.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then the surviving cherry deserves greater statistical weight.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S215 makes one marginal advantage outweigh every broader criterion brought into the decision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General competitive excuse-making can switch excuses; A repeatedly protects the same extra cherry as decisive.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 translates the cherry into B2's flavour criterion, and A5 increases that same cherry's weight after B4.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a imposes the tiny detail on scheduling choices; this alternative pressures a